t', 'kicker': 'Frieze Week 2019', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frieze Art Fair', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Virtual Reality (Computers)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kapoor, Anish', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rossin, Rachel (1987- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Randalls Island (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nathalie Djurberg', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hans Berg', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-01T14:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophie Haigney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haigney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/092a1848-3387-550e-a23d-e7c1fe81f5b5', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/092a1848-3387-550e-a23d-e7c1fe81f5b5'}, {'abstract': 'This month’s E.U. elections aren’t a fight between nationalists and progressives. They are total chaos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/01/opinion/european-elections.html', 'snippet': 'This month’s E.U. elections aren’t a fight between nationalists and progressives. They are total chaos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIENNA —\xa0In less than a month, Europeans will cast their ballots to elect the next European Parliament. If you read the Continent’s major newspapers and listen to political leaders, you will come to believe that the European electorate is radically polarized and voters are prepared to make a fateful choice. The elections this month, we often hear, will become a kind of referendum. The far right expects it to be a referendum on migration (or more accurately, on Brussels’s failure to deal with it), while progressive pro-Europeans foresee it as a referendum on the very survival of the European Union. Far-right strategists hope that the election will resemble Donald Trump’s 2016 victory, while pro-European progressives expect the elections to resemble the second round of the presidential vote in France in 2017, when Emmanuel Macron defeated Marine Le Pen. Both sides, we are told, agree on one thing: We face a tribal war between populist-nationalists and committed Europeanists.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1330, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/03/opinion/01krastev/01krastev-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',