 'subject', 'value': 'Russian Interference in 2016 US Elections and Ties to Trump Associates', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate Committee on the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barr, William P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-02T17:27:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Zraick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zraick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a14d8fa-0771-5ca4-9b73-9533730afbb6', 'word_count': 846, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a14d8fa-0771-5ca4-9b73-9533730afbb6'}, {'abstract': 'The improbably yet perfectly paired stars play old friends who unexpectedly find love in this charming (and a bit vulgar) romantic comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/02/movies/long-shot-review.html', 'snippet': 'The improbably yet perfectly paired stars play old friends who unexpectedly find love in this charming (and a bit vulgar) romantic comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Long Shot” isn’t going to save the romantic comedy, but it’s an adrenaline shot of pure pleasure to the genre’s failing heart. The outline is familiar: Two people meet, retreat and then circle each other, all while talking and talking. The romantic comedy turns on people who fit together — in bed, on the dance floor — but also talk to each other, exchanging words that stop flowing and faltering only with a culminating kiss and teasing fade-out. The difference here is that unlike a lot of romances, the woman retains her power and identity, and that’s how the movie likes it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'h