 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Rashbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd800689-f415-599a-9512-9dccb2817763', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd800689-f415-599a-9512-9dccb2817763'}, {'abstract': 'As the son of African immigrants, Antetokounmpo was unwelcome in Athens. Then he showed promise as a basketball star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/03/sports/giannis-antetokounmpo-greece.html', 'snippet': 'As the son of African immigrants, Antetokounmpo was unwelcome in Athens. Then he showed promise as a basketball star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATHENS — He is known as the Greek Freak, a basketball player of such transcendent ability that he has become celebrated as the face of the country of his birth. Yet for most of his life growing up in Greece, Giannis Antetokounmpo was considered a foreigner. As the son of African immigrants, he was perpetually vulnerable to attacks by racist militants, and to threats of deportation to Nigeria, a country he had never visited.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/05/sports/02giannis/merlin_153102705_50653696-fc62-402f-8c3f-d9fae4c8fcb9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/05/sports/02giannis/merlin_153102705_50653696-fc62-402f-8c3f-d9fae4c8fcb9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/05/sports/02giannis/merlin_153102705_50653696-fc62-402f-8c3f-d9f