[], 'pub_date': '2019-05-08T08:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Proulx', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Proulx', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46ce7751-3b2f-53d4-a64d-4425c4223246', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46ce7751-3b2f-53d4-a64d-4425c4223246'}, {'abstract': 'More than 30 painters examine themselves with unsparing honesty in a show of self-portraits bookended by Egon Schiele and Max Beckmann.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/arts/design/neue-galerie-self-portraits-review.html', 'snippet': 'More than 30 painters examine themselves with unsparing honesty in a show of self-portraits bookended by Egon Schiele and Max Beckmann.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A single self-portrait is an oddity — a titillating peek into an artist’s private life or self-conception, but otherwise no different from other figurative paintings. But when you see about 70 of them, by more than 30 artists, in “The Self-Portrait, From Schiele to Beckmann” at the Neue Galerie, you start to notice that this narrow genre has a universal scope. It’s a consciously constructed illusion of spontaneous self-revelation, a sincere put-on. And as such it’s a peek beneath the hood of art in general.  ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/10/arts/10neue1/merlin_153918459_21f2bfcc-e3e5-4325-82d7-c0536630a699-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/10/arts/10neue1/merlin_153918459_21f2bfcc-e3e5-4325-82d7-c0536630a699-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight