tname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'McAleavy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c37d025-15ee-519c-aa69-78a297e790fa', 'word_count': 1420, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c37d025-15ee-519c-aa69-78a297e790fa'}, {'abstract': 'A popular show gives “defector beauties” a chance to tell stories from the North. It’s loud and gaudy — and changing the conversation about reunification.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/08/magazine/korea-tv-reunification.html', 'snippet': 'A popular show gives “defector beauties” a chance to tell stories from the North. It’s loud and gaudy — and changing the conversation about reunification.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Episode 376 of “Now on My Way to Meet You” aired in late February, three days after the second summit meeting between Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un. Labeled a “special feature,” it began on the streets of Hanoi, a rare on-location shoot. One of the show’s two hosts, the genial comedian Nam Hee-seok, stood behind a police barricade with 30-year-old Shin Eun-ha, perhaps the most well known of the South Korean variety show’s recurring personalities, waiting for the arrival of Kim in an armored limousine. They were surrounded by reporters and gawkers holding up cellphone cameras to the North Korean entourage. When Kim’s limo appeared, Shin loudly, tearfully called out a plea: “Sir, please help me get back home! Please help me get back home!”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/12/magazine/12mag-defector/ac9a5ee269194e439e5588309bb3f1f8-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/12/magazine/12mag-defector/ac9a5ee269194e439e5588309bb3f1f8-3-articleLar