y', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-09T08:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Photo', 'section_name': 'Lens', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eudora Welty and Matthew Sedacca', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eudora', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Welty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sedacca', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02bc6750-02a6-5800-962c-b0decace4d41', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02bc6750-02a6-5800-962c-b0decace4d41'}, {'abstract': 'Too expensive to be self-sustaining, museums rely on collectors’ fortunes and corporate money. Now that money is being put under a microscope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/09/arts/design/museums-ethics.html', 'snippet': 'Too expensive to be self-sustaining, museums rely on collectors’ fortunes and corporate money. Now that money is being put under a microscope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For generations Americans tended to see art museums as alternatives to crass everyday life. Like libraries, they were for learning; like churches, for reflection. You went to them for a hit of Beauty and a lesson in “eternal values,” embodied in relics of the past donated by civic-minded angels.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/12/arts/12moralmuseum1/merlin_150532329_eaf4c084-d778-42b8-b787-a21919f0dd7e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/12/arts/12moralmuseum1/merlin_150532329_eaf4c084-d778-42b8-b787-a21919f0dd7e-articleLar