': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d713f85-7bb6-5e6b-8dbc-21c97e408d76', 'word_count': 1825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d713f85-7bb6-5e6b-8dbc-21c97e408d76'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon refuse to reinforce our bad behaviors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/11/crosswords/variety-acrostic.html', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon refuse to reinforce our bad behaviors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ACROSTIC  — Today’s passage is by Amy Sutherland, from a book called “What Shamu Taught Me About Life, Love and Marriage: Lessons for People From Animals and Their Trainers.” If anyone hears a bell ringing, Ms. Sutherland is a journalist who wrote the most-emailed article of the year for nytimes.com, back in 2006, on training her husband using techniques developed for other more exotic animals. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: Acrostic', 'kicker': 'SUNDAY VARIETY COLUMN', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Behavior', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cox, Emily (Crossword Constructor)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rathvon, Henry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-11T15:40:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Lovinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/728128ff-8ee0-528d-82ba-5490e456544a', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/728128ff-8ee0-528d-82ba-5490e456544a'}, {'abstract': 'Plant and animal species are disappearing faster than at any time in recorded history. We know who is to blame.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/11/opinion/sunday/extinction-endangered-species-biodiversity.html', 'snippet': 'Plant and animal species are disappearing faster than at any time in recorded history. We know who is to blame.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our planet has suffered five mass extinctions, the last of which occurred about 66 million years ago, when a giant asteroid believed to have landed near the Yucatán Peninsula set off a chain reaction that wiped out the dinosaurs and roughly three-quarters of the other species on earth. A few years ago, in a book called “The Sixth Extinction,” the writer Elizabeth Kolbert warned of a devastating sequel, with plant and animal species on land and sea already disappearing at a ferocious clip, their habitats destroyed or diminished by human activities.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/12/opinion/11species/445e245619ff45d99ac2e0489435e87c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 465, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/12/opinion/11species/445e245619ff45d99ac2e0489435e87c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 465}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/12/opinion/11species/445e245619ff45d99ac2e0489435e87c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 794, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/12/opinion/11species/445e245619ff45d99ac2e048943