: 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bashir, Omar Hassan Al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sudan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-13T17:20:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Declan Walsh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Declan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walsh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4a851d2-7221-5a13-90e2-2c6181349245', 'word_count': 1013, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4a851d2-7221-5a13-90e2-2c6181349245'}, {'abstract': 'Architecture can be declared a landmark, but all too often the fate of artwork that has been made for a particular environment remains uncertain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/13/t-magazine/site-specific-art.html', 'snippet': 'Architecture can be declared a landmark, but all too often the fate of artwork that has been made for a particular environment remains uncertain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LAST JULY, AFTER months of frantic protests and petitions, Philip Johnson’s audacious postmodern icon on Madison Avenue, the AT&T skyscraper known for its gigantic arch and notched Chippendale roof, became a landmark, a designation that blocked proposed changes to the facade. Architecture fans rejoiced, but no one said anything about the second-floor lobby, where a pair of radiant red and gold frescoes, two of the artist Dorothea Rockburne’s most important works, remained. The Olayan Group, the investors who own the building, have so far agreed only to communicate with Rockburne, who currently has a long-term installation at Dia:Beacon in upstate New York, through a second party. The firm says her paintings are still in place, “fully protected,” and that they are “safeguarding their future,” but Rockburne doubts they will survive the interior renovations that were announced in December. According to the artist, there is nowhere else for the murals to go.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '112', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 489, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 489}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1668, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/13/t-magazine/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01/13tmag-sitespecific-slide-4X01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 1