rial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b497fcd8-0b6d-5c81-84d3-b683c4f3b01e', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b497fcd8-0b6d-5c81-84d3-b683c4f3b01e'}, {'abstract': 'The online left doesn’t like Joe Biden. Voters seem to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/13/opinion/joe-biden-twitter-2020.html', 'snippet': 'The online left doesn’t like Joe Biden. Voters seem to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Twitter during late 2015 and early 2016, Donald Trump’s front-runner status was evident, even if it hadn’t yet fully sunk in with the tribunes of conventional wisdom. Trump’s following far outstripped his rivals. His tweets drove news cycles, and channeled the resentments of a furious base. In October 2015, The New York Times described Twitter as a “powerful bulwark” of support during a shaky moment in Trump’s campaign, noting that he was retweeted twice as often as Hillary Clinton and 13 times more than Jeb Bush. The platform helped make him president.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/15/opinion/13goldbergWeb/merlin_154388052_e148992d-91e9-4987-8029-9722ceba9855-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/15/opinion/13goldbergWeb/merlin_154388052_e148992d-91e9-4987-8029-9722ceba9855-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/15/opinion/13goldbergWeb/merlin_154388052_e148992d-91e9-4987-8029-9722ceba9855-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/15/opinion/13goldbergWeb/merlin_154388052_e148992d-91e9-4987-8029-9722ceba9855-superJumbo