e, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/57e9fbc5-772e-5d2d-b0ff-201ec83328bf', 'word_count': 1441, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57e9fbc5-772e-5d2d-b0ff-201ec83328bf'}, {'abstract': 'Handmade pastas from a Osteria Francescana chef, a five-course tasting menu in the West Village and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/14/dining/nyc-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'Handmade pastas from a Osteria Francescana chef, a five-course tasting menu in the West Village and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has been known, for years, that the original Essex Market, founded in 1940, one of several city markets established by Mayor Fiorello H. LaGuardia, would have to shut its doors, giving way to more lucrative use of its real estate. Its much larger replacement has opened across Delancey Street in a vast new multifaceted development called Essex Crossing. Run by the New York City Economic Development Corporation, the market covers 37,000 square feet, triple the original’s size, and has added 16 new vendors to the old market’s 21, along with two restaurant spaces and a demonstration kitchen. Shoppers can stock up on ingredients from grocers, fishmongers, cheese purveyors, butchers and others. They can also sample Middle Eastern specialties from Samesa; seafood from Don Ceviche; Moroccan fare by Zerza; meat from Essex Shambles; Thai chicken and rice from Eat Gai; and sweets by Lower East Side Ice Cream Factory and Josephine’s Feast, all new to the market. Later this year, Roni Mazumdar, an owner of the Indian restaurants Adda and Rahi, will open Dhamaka, a full-service restaurant serving regional Indian cuisine. A huge lower-level market, not just for food, called Market Line is also set to open at Essex Crossing in late summer.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York 