United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Prosecutors (Independent Counsel)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Agents Registration Act', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Calendars', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANDRES, GREG D', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldstein, Andrew D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueller, Robert S III', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weissmann, Andrew', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-15T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Goldman, Michael S. Schmidt and Noah Weiland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Noah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ba9e052-1426-53cb-b3a1-459b502d9762', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ba9e052-1426-53cb-b3a1-459b502d9762'}, {'abstract': 'South of Marrakesh, the Draa Valley still exerts an indefinable pull, retaining traces of its now almost-vanished Berber kingdom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/15/t-magazine/morocco-travel-draa-valley.html', 'snippet': 'South of Marrakesh, the Draa Valley still exerts an indefinable pull, retaining traces of its now almost-vanished Berber kingdom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SHAMROCK GREEN of Casablanca graded into a flat plain of beige. From the tarmac itself, I could see the beige run into a towering wall of white — the Atlas Mountains. Edith Wharton, in her 1920 travelogue, “In Morocco,” had felt herself fall under the spell of the Atlas and the desert beyond as well. “Unknown Africa,” she writes, “seems much nearer to Morocco than to the white towns of Tunis and the smiling oases of South Algeria. One feels the nearness of Marrakech at Fez, and at Marrakech that of Timbuctoo.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/14/t-magazine/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B/14tmag-morocco-slide-H17B-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thu