siness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeanna Smialek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smialek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52dabf0e-d03b-57aa-a086-5d541f645466', 'word_count': 986, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52dabf0e-d03b-57aa-a086-5d541f645466'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of baseball’s richest teams dominating smaller-market clubs, Tampa Bay has managed a third way: spending less and winning more through ceaseless innovation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/16/sports/tampa-bay-rays.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of baseball’s richest teams dominating smaller-market clubs, Tampa Bay has managed a third way: spending less and winning more through ceaseless innovation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — A mile and a half from the locus of baseball surrealism is a monument to the master of the medium. The Dalí Museum, home to more than 2,000 works by the Spanish artist Salvador Dalí, makes a fitting neighbor for the Tampa Bay Rays.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/merlin_154926798_333c11e4-3960-4d68-afcd-49c24c36bfcf-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/merlin_154926798_333c11e4-3960-4d68-afcd-49c24c36bfcf-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/merlin_154926798_333c11e4-3960-4d68-afcd-49c24c36bfcf-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/merlin_154926798_333c11e4-3960-4d68-afcd-49c24c36bfcf-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/16rays-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/16rays-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/sports/17rays-print/16rays-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rays Are a Surrealist’s Delight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Rays Embrace Their Weirdness, and Success Follows', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snell, Blake (1992- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-16T16:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5fe8014-4aa9-5cc7-ab30-cc673073619c', 'word_count': 1867, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5fe8014-4aa9-5cc7-ab30-cc673073619c'}, {'abstract': 'Seven well-traveled writers discuss the dilemma of whether or not to visit nations with oppressive governments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/16/t-magazine/ethical-travel-reporting.html', 'snippet': 'Seven well-traveled writers discuss the dilemma of whether or not to visit nations with oppressive governments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What should one consider when choosing where to travel? Every destination offers its own appealing reasons for visiting — from its food to its people, from its cities to its historical sites — and there are always the more practical concerns that weigh down any traveler (costs, visas, seasons, flight routes, internet access). But the 21st-century traveler is more knowledgeable than ever before — she must engage in the ethics of where she visits, and why. Global conflicts are not new, but in a shrunken world, we hear of them much more quickly and in much more detail than we would have a generation ago. This means that even the casual traveler also faces an ethical dilemma when she chooses where to visit. What about a country’s treatment of minorities? What about its freedom of speech or the transparency of its government? And if we do go to a country ruled by a despot or military junta, will our currency benefit the nation’s citizens or only the regime that oppresses them? While there are few widely agreed upon rules for answering these questions, asking them seems increasingly vital. Here, seven travel writers contemplate the ethical ambiguities of travel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/16/t-magazine/travel/travel-sidebar-slide-QFMJ/travel-sidebar-slide-QFMJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/16/t-magazine/travel/travel-sidebar-slide-QFMJ/travel-sidebar-slide-QFMJ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 