 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Koblin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koblin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04be0177-4770-503c-8a02-4604f18e8e03', 'word_count': 289, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04be0177-4770-503c-8a02-4604f18e8e03'}, {'abstract': 'Why “Rabbit,” the perfect art for the roaring mid-80s, continues to speak to us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/17/arts/jeff-koons-auction-christies.html', 'snippet': 'Why “Rabbit,” the perfect art for the roaring mid-80s, continues to speak to us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeff Koons is back on top, if on top means holding the highest auction price for a living artist, as hyped by the auction house responsible. Mr. Koons’s 1986 “Rabbit,” a precise stainless steel copy of a plastic inflatable toy — mirror-smooth yet with seams and puckers — sold Wednesday night at Christie’s Post-War and Contemporary Art sale for $91.1 million, the highlight of New York’s buoyant spring auctions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1453, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/17/arts/17koons1/17koons1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stop Hating Jeff Koons', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stop Hating Jeff Koons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Christie's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koons, Jeff', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-17T19:24:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0530d62d-0582-5d14-b0a8-442702c91cbc', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0530d62d-0582-5d14-b0a8-442702c91cbc'}, {'abstract': 'Shamel Pitts and Bobbi Jene Smith, veterans of Ohad Naharin’s company, showed their distinctive dance voices in New York performances.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/17/arts/dance/shamel-pitts-black-velvet-bobbi-jene-smith-lost-mountain.html', 'snippet': 'Shamel Pitts and Bobbi Jene Smith, veterans of Ohad Naharin’s company, showed their distinctive dance voices in New York performances.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fifteen years ago, the choreographers Bobbi Jene Smith and Shamel Pitts were classmates at Juilliard. Ms. Smith left before graduating to join the Batsheva Dance Company in Tel Aviv; Mr. Pitts arrived at the same company four years later. As friends, they collaborated on “Arrowed,” a project initiated by Ms. Smith in which they sit face to face and interrogate each other. The two left Batsheva not far apart, Ms. Smith in 2014, Mr. Pitts in 2016, to make work on their own.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/20/arts/17pitts-smith-1/merlin_154908117_93e6aa7a-3c9b-4ae8-82da-264a5a5bc04b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/20/arts/17pitts-smith-1/merlin_154908117_93e6aa7a-3c9b-4ae8-82da-264a5a5bc04b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/20/arts/17pitts-smith-1/merlin_154908117_93e6aa7a-3c9b-4ae8-82da-264a5a5bc04b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/20/arts/17pitts-smith-1/merlin_154908117_93e6aa7a-3c9b-4ae8-82da-264a5a5bc04b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/20/arts/17pitts-smith-1/17pitts-smith-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'he