subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Haberman and Annie Karni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Karni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a5b5276-ee10-5a4e-81e2-b135b4d2d46f', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a5b5276-ee10-5a4e-81e2-b135b4d2d46f'}, {'abstract': 'Are we writing for other disabled people, for the nondisabled, or for everyone? The work of these poets speaks for itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/19/opinion/disability-poems.html', 'snippet': 'Are we writing for other disabled people, for the nondisabled, or for everyone? The work of these poets speaks for itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The seven poems you will find here below, as well as seven others that will follow in a second post, were curated by us over the past several months, and represent only a small segment of poets with disabilities writing vital, engaged and powerful work today. To arrive here, we asked many disabled poets to offer their work, and this process raised issues that permeate debates in the disability community. We learned that some poets prefer not to claim disability as an identity, publicly or at all, and because this is a “disability” series, those poets did not wish to offer their work. We were also faced with questions raised in the recent and provocative debate between “disability poetics” and “crip poetics”: Who is the audience? Are we writing for other disabled people? For the nondisabled, or for everyone? How do we write for both while emphasizing the disabled poet’s aesthetic?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1396, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/06/11/opinion/11disability-poems1-5/a274e9949a2343fdac5c006ddbcb414e-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Make No Apologies for Yourself’', 'kicker': 'disability', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-