'value': 'Fosbury, Dick (1947- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holzhauer, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jeopardy! (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-20T23:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Asher Price', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Asher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Price', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/62562e7c-202f-5c87-b662-6ad5b0b3e9fb', 'word_count': 958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62562e7c-202f-5c87-b662-6ad5b0b3e9fb'}, {'abstract': 'Once again, analysts overestimated the resonance of climate change and underestimated the power of economic populism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/opinion/australia-election-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'Once again, analysts overestimated the resonance of climate change and underestimated the power of economic populism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was another election that couldn’t be lost until it was. Rived by years of infighting, Australia’s conservative governing coalition was trailing in the polls. The opposition Labor Party’s polls showed it all but certain of ousting Prime Minister Scott Morrison, and its action platform on climate change seemed bound to resonate in a country devastated by drought, heat waves, brush fires and the loss of its magnificent Great Barrier Reef to warming seas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/20/opinion/20australia1/20australia1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/20/opinion/20australia1/20australia1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 38