: 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3eec3ad-3a9f-5b1a-93e5-70440bd365fb', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3eec3ad-3a9f-5b1a-93e5-70440bd365fb'}, {'abstract': "Vintage photos of New York's hometown amusement park", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/nyregion/joy-and-fear-on-coney-islands-emotional-roller-coaster.html', 'snippet': "Vintage photos of New York's hometown amusement park", 'lead_paragraph': 'In May 1914, Coney Island played host to an unlikely party of VIPs led by Sir Arthur Conan Doyle and his wife, Lady Doyle. A New York Times reporter trailed the group all day, hoping for a quote from the famed creator of Sherlock Holmes. “First he shot the chutes, then he took the seemingly perilous Whip ride, and finally he went into the ridiculous Crazy Village,” wrote the dutiful journalist. “And he enjoyed it all — particularly the Whip, which he pronounced thrilling.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 589, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1179, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/lens/22coney-ast-1/22coney-ast-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Coney Island’s Rides Have Delighted (and Frightened) Us for Decades', 'kicker': 'Past Tense', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Coney Island (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roller Coasters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doyle, Arthur Conan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mailer, Norman', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-20T23:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Giles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Giles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a9b57c0-74d8-5395-aa54-18ce3bb662d7', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a9b57c0-74d8-5395-aa54-18ce3bb662d7'}, {'abstract': 'Morgenster extra-virgin olive oil is made on a wine estate on the Cape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/dining/south-african-olive-oil.html', 'snippet': 'Morgenster extra-virgin olive oil is made on a wine estate on the Cape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STELLENBOSCH, South Africa — For fine olive oil, South Africa does not readily come to mind. But the Mediterranean climate of the Cape wine region is excellent for olive trees, as Giulio Bertrand, an Italian who settled in Stellenbosch in the 1990s, discovered. Today it is estimated that the original plot — “the mother block” of trees on his estate, Morgenster — has provided millions of olive trees to growers in Namibia, South Africa and Zimbabwe, essentially kick-starting olive oil production throughout Southern Africa. Now, there are about 20 brands of extra-virgin olive oil in South Africa. Mr. Bertrand died last year at 91, but his daughters, Federica and Alessandra Bertrand, continue to run the estate, which has 30,000 trees and also produces wines. And the olive oils are perennial prizewinners. The 2018 bottling was ranked among the best in the world by Flos Olei, an olive oil guidebook. The 2018 oil is the estate’s flagship blend of 14 varietals, each picked and pressed separately before being combined. The extra-virgin oil is richly herbaceous on the palate, with notes of pepper and pleasing bitterness.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1103, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1103}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 558, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1115, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner4/20burner4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Olive Oil From South Africa', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Drizzle: South African Olive Oil That’ll Please Your Palate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olive Oil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgenster Estate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-20T23:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5209548-816c-5742-ab59-9dba339cd10c', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5209548-816c-5742-ab59-9dba339cd10c'}, {'abstract': 'Albert Trummer, who first tended bar at David Bouley’s Danube, will lead three classes on cocktails this summer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/dining/cocktail-classes-albert-trummer.html', 'snippet': 'Albert Trummer, who first tended bar at David Bouley’s Danube, will lead three classes on cocktails this summer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Starting next month, the mixologist Albert Trummer will be giving classes on making summer drinks, using mostly organic and biodynamic ingredients. His base of operations will be Bouley at Home, thanks to the 20-year relationship that he has had with the chef David Bouley. (Mr. Trummer first stood behind the bar at Mr. Bouley’s Danube.) The class called Summer Beginnings will be held on June 26 ($125). Midsummer Night Mixology is scheduled for July 17 ($150), and Sayonara Summer Fête will be Aug. 21 ($150). All classes run from 7 to 10 p.m., accompanied by hors d’oeuvres from Mr. Bouley’s kitchen.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner2/20burner2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cocktail Classes at Bouley at Home', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Mix: Cocktail Classes At Bouley at Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails and Mixed Drinks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bouley at Home (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trummer, Albert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-20T23:04:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/133025c5-d776-55d5-9909-d4a418510910', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/133025c5-d776-55d5-9909-d4a418510910'}, {'abstract': 'In his new book, the chef gives practical advice for working with broccoli, asparagus and beets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/dining/jose-andres-vegetables.html', 'snippet': 'In his new book, the chef gives practical advice for working with broccoli, asparagus and beets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'José Andrés, the chef, restaurateur and philanthropist, sets off on an extravagant vegetable journey in his dazzling new cookbook by stating that he is not a vegetarian — “not even close.” A home cook has much to learn, though, from a professional about making the most of vegetables. Boil your vegetables in water, he says, but taste to make sure each is properly done. Keep frozen peas and canned white asparagus on hand. Simply roast broccoli florets. Slather asparagus with miso for grilling. Make a “bouillabaisse” with fennel, potatoes, tomatoes and rouille but no fish. A “poke” bowl features beets instead of tuna. There are recipes for some Spanish classics, including gazpacho (with many variations). He gives advice on cocktails and vegetable scraps and occasionally slips in eggs, cheese and bacon. Woven throughout are profiles of farmers.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 280}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 478, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 956, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner3/20burner3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The José Andrés Approach to Vegetables', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Prepare: The José Andrés  Approach to Vegetables', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Andres, Jose (1969- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Vegetables Unleashed: A Cookbook (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-20T23:08:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/198737b7-b57c-5826-90a6-c769fcd16278', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/198737b7-b57c-5826-90a6-c769fcd16278'}, {'abstract': 'Give him the gift of meat. A Korean barbecue restaurant sells over-the-top short-rib box sets in time for Father’s Day.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/20/dining/korean-barbecue-short-ribs.html', 'snippet': 'Give him the gift of meat. A Korean barbecue restaurant sells over-the-top short-rib box sets in time for Father’s Day.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a South Korean tradition of giving special cuts of meat that have been elaborately wrapped in silk for special occasions. Now, a classic Korean barbecue restaurant on West 32nd Street has started selling these gift packages, all with the restaurant’s prime short ribs three ways: marinated, bone-in and boneless. Though short ribs are usually meant for braising, the marinated ones have been scored for tenderness. The boneless and bone-in are thin enough to be succulent after a quick sear. Cooking suggestions are included. A small package with six portions is $250; a package for 12 people is $350; and the large (serving 24, with eight pounds of meat) is $450. All are available for pickup at the restaurant; delivery will be available next month.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 503, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 503}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 774, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/22/dining/20burner1/20burner1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name':