iefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/6123d09f-3d46-5d56-8f00-b5e6026c2e12', 'word_count': 1409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6123d09f-3d46-5d56-8f00-b5e6026c2e12'}, {'abstract': 'Our critic says the film plays like a love letter to — and requiem for — the industry the movie’s director loves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/21/movies/quentin-tarantino-once-upon-a-time-in-hollywood.html', 'snippet': 'Our critic says the film plays like a love letter to — and requiem for — the industry the movie’s director loves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When news broke that Quentin Tarantino would be taking on the Manson murders in his latest film, I admittedly winced. Tarantino’s love for over-the-top gore, for painting the screen red, seemed a bad fit with the ghastly 1969 murders of several people, including the actress Sharon Tate, then married to Roman Polanski. What was entirely unexpected was that “Once Upon a Time ... in Hollywood,” which had its world premiere at the Cannes Film Festival on Tuesday, would be such a moving film, at once a love letter — and a dream — of the Hollywood that was.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/21/arts/21cannes-tarantino/21cannes-tarantino-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/21/arts/21cannes-tarantino/21cannes-tarantino-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/21/arts/21cannes-tarantino/21cannes-tarantino-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/21/arts/21cannes-tarantino/21cannes-tarantino-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 