': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/05/24/opinion/21konstandaras-2/21konstandaras-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/24/opinion/21konstandaras-2/21konstandaras-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Is Playing Politics With the Port of Piraeus?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHAEOLOGY AND ANTHROPOLOGY', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Building (Construction)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'COSCO Group (China Ocean Shipping Co)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greek Culture Ministry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coalition of the Radical Left (Greece)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsipras, Alexis', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Macedonia', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-23T05:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nikos Konstandaras', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nikos', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Konstandaras', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d8a1767-13f0-5b27-8a2a-fd404aeb8d58', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d8a1767-13f0-5b27-8a2a-fd404aeb8d58'}, {'abstract': 'John Turturro stars in a new adaptation of the Umberto Eco novel. And “Elementary” begins its final season on CBS.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/23/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday-the-name-of-the-rose-and-elementary.html', 'snippet': 'John Turturro stars in a new adaptation of the Umberto Eco novel. And “Elementary” begins its final season on CBS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE NAME OF THE ROSE 10 p.m. on SundanceTV. With “Game of Thrones” having heaved its last breath of dragon fire on  Sunday, many have been left looking for a new series to fill the sword-shaped void. One holier option is this medieval mystery mini-series, the adaptation of Umberto Eco’s best-known novel, “The Name of the Rose,” by the  Italian director Giacomo Battiato. The book, which came out in 1980,  sold millions of copies, and was the basis for a 1986 film adaptation with Sean Connery. The role Connery played, a Franciscan friar named William of Baskerville, is here played by John Turturro. The plot involves  mysterious deaths at a remote abbey. When the Connery movie came out, Vincent Canby said in his review for The New York Times that it had cut out the novel’s scholarly asides, which “cover everything from the political maneuvering within the Avignon papacy, the fight for the Holy Roman Empire and the church’s merciless suppression of heresies (Waldensian, Albigensian and Catharist, among others) to the invention of eyeglasses and gunpowder.” The mini-series format might make room for more of Eco’s embellishments onscreen.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/23/arts/23tvcol-nameoftherose/23tvcol-nameoftherose-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'im