ew York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Richard Holbrooke ‘Our Man’ or His Own Man?', 'kicker': 'Letters to the Editor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-24T14:56:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/913a3535-e425-5fa5-9627-1cc1b653909c', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/913a3535-e425-5fa5-9627-1cc1b653909c'}, {'abstract': 'We humans know more truths than any species on earth. Yet we also believe the most falsehoods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/24/opinion/why-fiction-trumps-truth.html', 'snippet': 'We humans know more truths than any species on earth. Yet we also believe the most falsehoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many people believe that truth conveys power. If some leaders, religions or ideologies misrepresent reality, they will eventually lose to more clearsighted rivals. Hence sticking with the truth is the best strategy for gaining power. Unfortunately, this is just a comforting myth. In fact, truth and power have a far more complicated relationship, because in human society, power means two very different things.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/26/opinion/sunday/26PowerHarari/4e181267a53c4a8f831f60f47899a0c7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/26/opinion/sunday/26PowerHarari/4e181267a53c4a8f831f60f47899a0c7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp