k': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-26T04:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97de9960-91d8-5baa-849f-8e86fa1b039c', 'word_count': 7276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97de9960-91d8-5baa-849f-8e86fa1b039c'}, {'abstract': '“Vida” returns for a second season on Starz. And a “Game of Thrones” documentary airs on HBO.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/26/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday-vida-and-game-of-thrones-the-last-watch.html', 'snippet': '“Vida” returns for a second season on Starz. And a “Game of Thrones” documentary airs on HBO.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIDA 9 p.m. on Starz. When the first season of this drama began last year, the two sisters at its center, Emma (Mishel Prada) and Lyn (Melissa Barrera), returned to the Eastside Los Angeles neighborhood they grew up in, navigating a transformed neighborhood in which they both felt like outsiders. That season ended with the pair committing to taking over the family-owned bar that had been passed to them from their mother. The second season, available in its entirety on Starz streaming platforms and airing its first episode on the network Sunday night, continues the show’s interest in investigating gentrification and cultural identity. “In this season of ‘Vida’ I wanted to touch on the notion of being prieto, and how colorism is alive and well in the Latinx community,” Tanya Saracho, the showrunner of “Vida,” told The New York Times, using a gender-neutral term for Latinos. “All that is our shame, our stuff that we haven’t aired out that much. And I love when we get to air it out.”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/26/arts/26tvcol-vida/26tvc