5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-05-30T20:12:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kari Molvar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Molvar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a64a854-c16a-5997-83a3-eedf4eec2a91', 'word_count': 751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a64a854-c16a-5997-83a3-eedf4eec2a91'}, {'abstract': 'With support for the party cratering, its political leader, Luigi Di Maio, survived a confidence vote. The movement’s prospects, however, are far from clear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/30/world/europe/italy-five-star-di-maio-confidence-vote.html', 'snippet': 'With support for the party cratering, its political leader, Luigi Di Maio, survived a confidence vote. The movement’s prospects, however, are far from clear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — Not long ago, the Five Star movement looked like the power to be reckoned with in Italian politics. The internet-based party stormed the political establishment last year to become the dominant force in Italy’s government.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/30/world/30italy1/merlin_155371431_9925740b-7248-417e-a495-4c2d950a3c89-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/05/30/world/30italy1/merlin_155371431_9925740b-7248-417e-a495-4c2d950a3c89-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/05/30/world/30italy1/merlin_155371431_9925740b-7248-417e-a495-4c2d950a3c89-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 1024, 'subTy